    id: 'mario',
    name: 'Super Mario',
    developer: 'Nintendo',
    since: 1981,
    description: 'The best-selling video game franchise of all time, with more than 900 million copies sold. Mario debuted as Jumpman in Donkey Kong (1981) and has since appeared in more than 200 games across many genres.',
    longDescription: 'Mario began as the hero of Donkey Kong (1981), the arcade game Shigeru Miyamoto designed after the failure of Radar Scope. The character was Jumpman in the game, but some overseas promotional material called him Mario, after Mario Segale, the landlord of Nintendo of America\'s office and warehouse. Accounts of why differ: one has Segale interrupting a meeting to demand overdue rent, while former warehouse manager Don James said he and Minoru Arakawa picked the name as a joke, because Segale was so reclusive that no employee had ever met him. Donkey Kong Jr. (1982) made Mario the villain, and Mario Bros. (1983) was the first game with his name in the title and the first to feature Luigi.\n\nSuper Mario Bros. (1985), the pack-in game for the NES, made him a star. It has sold more than 58 million copies, it helped popularise the side-scrolling platform game, and, alongside the console, it is credited as a key factor in reviving the industry after the 1983 crash. The sequel Japan received was released internationally as The Lost Levels, while the West got a different Super Mario Bros. 2 in 1988. Super Mario 64 (1996), the series\' first fully 3D game and a Nintendo 64 launch title, did for 3D what the original had done for side-scrolling: its camera system and 360-degree analog control set a new archetype, and it sold nearly twelve million copies by 2015.\n\nThe franchise then spread well beyond platformers, into subseries such as Mario Kart, Mario Party, Mario Tennis, Mario Golf and Paper Mario, while Luigi, Wario and Yoshi went on to lead successful series of their own. Mario has sold more than 900 million copies in all, more than 430 million of them in the core Super Mario games, and it has reached cinemas in 1993, 2023 and 2026 and theme parks as Super Nintendo World.',
