    id: 'ultima',
    name: 'Ultima',
    developer: 'Origin Systems',
    since: 1981,
    description: 'Richard Garriott\'s series helped establish the norms of the computer RPG. Ultima IV replaced the villain with a test of eight virtues, and Ultima Online became one of the earliest and longest-running successful MMORPGs.',
    longDescription: 'Ultima came from Richard Garriott, and is sometimes traced back to his Akalabeth: World of Doom (1979). Alongside Wizardry and Might and Magic, it is considered to have established many norms of the computer role-playing game. Garriott later described the first three games, Ultima I (1981) through Exodus (1983), as "Richard Garriott learns to program", though Exodus introduced the party system used by many later games. He also changed how games were sold. Disliking zip lock bags with a few printed pages, he insisted Ultima II come in a box with a cloth map and a manual, and Sierra was the only company then willing to agree. Every main game after it came with a cloth map, and from Ultima IV on small trinkets such as pendants and coins joined them.\n\nUltima IV: Quest of the Avatar (1985) broke with hero-against-villain plots. Lord British summons the player not to defeat a villain but to lead by spiritual example, and a system of Eight Virtues, derived from Love, Truth and Courage, measures how close their actions come to that ideal. The next two games turned the idea on itself. In Ultima V (1988), Lord Blackthorn rules in the king\'s absence and enforces a twisted version of the Virtues, and in Ultima VI (1990) the invading Gargoyles prove to have valid reasons to loathe the Avatar, in a story that explores racism and xenophobia.\n\nUltima VII: The Black Gate (1992) is widely lauded as a high point. Almost anything not nailed down can be moved or used: players can bake bread, forge weapons, play instruments and paint a self-portrait. It was also, Garriott said, the first game he planned ahead for, since VII to IX were a preplanned trilogy; he called IV to VI a "backwards-designed" one. Electronic Arts has owned the brand since 1992. Ultima VIII (1994) missed sales expectations, and Ultima IX (1999) ended the main series. Ultima Online (1997), partly designed as a social and economic experiment, became an unexpected hit, but its planned successors were cancelled in 2001 and 2004. Garriott no longer owns the series, though he retains rights to several of its characters, so neither he nor EA can make a new Ultima without the other\'s permission.',
