    id: 'kings-quest',
    name: "King's Quest",
    developer: 'Sierra On-Line',
    since: 1984,
    description: 'Roberta Williams\'s fairy-tale adventure series began in 1984 as a showcase for IBM\'s PCjr. Its pseudo-3D scenes, where the hero could walk behind objects, helped build the reputation of Sierra On-Line.',
    longDescription: 'King\'s Quest began when IBM approached Sierra to make a game like Wizard and the Princess (1980) that would show off the animation and graphics of its upcoming PCjr. Roberta Williams, Sierra\'s co-founder, designed it as a blend of common fairy tales and finished it over 18 months with a team of six programmers and artists, who had to develop new techniques along the way. The result, released in 1984, sent young knight Sir Graham across the realm of Daventry in search of three magical items, through flip-screen locations drawn in a pseudo-3D style, using typed commands. It was a bestseller, with more than 100,000 copies sold by 1986. Critics applauded the advance on text and static images, though some found it slow and very difficult.\n\nWilliams loved fairy tales, the Andrew Lang Fairy Books in particular, and many puzzles draw on them, so a player who knew the stories had an advantage. The series follows the royal family of Daventry over two generations. Graham wins the crown, then searches for his queen, Valanice, in King\'s Quest II. In King\'s Quest III the hero is Gwydion, a slave of the wizard Manannan, who turns the wizard\'s magic against him and discovers he is Alexander, the long-lost son of Graham and Valanice. King\'s Quest IV sends Princess Rosella to the land of Tamir for a cure for her dying father, and King\'s Quest VI follows Alexander to the Land of the Green Isles.\n\nLater games moved further from that formula. King\'s Quest VII: The Princeless Bride is the only entry without Graham, with Valanice and Rosella as joint leads, and Mask of Eternity (1998) stars Connor, a tanner unrelated to the royal family. Williams designed every game until the episodic re-imagining by The Odd Gentlemen (2015–2016). The series was primarily responsible for building Sierra\'s reputation, and its characters turned up as in-jokes across the company\'s other series, including Space Quest, Police Quest and Leisure Suit Larry.',
    titleKeyword: "King's Quest",
    developerKeyword: 'Sierra',
