    id: 'resident-evil',
    name: 'Resident Evil',
    developer: 'Capcom',
    since: 1996,
    description: 'Capcom\'s horror series defined survival horror with the 1996 original, then popularised the over-the-shoulder view with Resident Evil 4. With more than 218 million games sold, it is Capcom\'s best-selling franchise.',
    longDescription: 'Resident Evil began in 1993, when Capcom\'s Tokuro Fujiwara told Shinji Mikami and his colleagues to make a game using elements of Fujiwara\'s 1989 Famicom game Sweet Home. Directed by Mikami, it went through several redesigns, first as a Super NES game and then as a first-person PlayStation game, before settling on a third-person view. Its Japanese title, Biohazard, could not easily be secured in the US, where a DOS game and a New York hardcore punk band already used it, so a staff contest produced Resident Evil. Released in 1996, it was the first game dubbed "survival horror" and established the inventory and save systems the genre would use. Mikami felt its fixed cameras and "tank controls" made it scarier, and some critics have argued the clumsiness was deliberate.\n\nResident Evil 2 (1998) and Resident Evil 3: Nemesis (1999) followed on the PlayStation, and the first two games had sold about 11 million units by March 1999. Resident Evil 4 had a harder birth. Development began in 1999 for the PlayStation 2 and four proposed versions were discarded; one, directed by Hideki Kamiya, felt too far from the series and became Devil May Cry (2001). Mikami finally took over as director, and the 2005 GameCube release replaced fixed cameras with a tracking view that moved over the shoulder to aim. Its approach spread to games such as Gears of War, Dead Space and The Last of Us, and it had sold 15.2 million copies by March 2025.\n\nThe series then leaned further into action. Resident Evil 5 (2009) added cooperative play and became the franchise\'s best-seller at the time, despite a mixed fan reception, and Resident Evil 6 (2012) was reviewed unevenly. In 2013 producer Masachika Kawata promised a return to horror and suspense, delivered by the first-person Resident Evil 7: Biohazard (2017) and Resident Evil Village (2021). Remakes of Resident Evil 2, 3 and 4 appeared from 2019, the ninth main game, Requiem, arrived in February 2026, and sales stood at more than 218 million copies by October 2026.',
