    id: 'metal-gear',
    name: 'Metal Gear',
    developer: 'Konami',
    since: 1987,
    description: 'Hideo Kojima\'s series is credited with pioneering and popularising the stealth game. It began on the MSX2 in 1987, when hardware limits pushed him away from combat, and Metal Gear Solid (1998) gave it international fame.',
    longDescription: 'Metal Gear was meant to be an action game about modern military combat, but the MSX2 could show only a limited number of bullets and enemies on screen, which Hideo Kojima felt undermined the fighting. Inspired by The Great Escape, he refocused it on a prisoner escaping, and the stealth game grew from there. Released in 1987 for MSX2 computers, it sent Solid Snake after the walking nuclear tank of its title, in a Cold War story about politicians of East and West manipulating soldiers, set against the idea of Outer Heaven, a country without politics.\n\nThe series soon went on without him. A separate team built a heavily modified NES port, and Konami followed it with an NES sequel, Snake\'s Revenge (1990), again without Kojima. One of that game\'s designers met him and asked him to make a "real Metal Gear sequel", and his answer was Metal Gear 2: Solid Snake, released the same year for the MSX2 in Japan. A third game was planned for the 3DO in 1994 before moving to the PlayStation, where the word "Solid" marked the shift to 3D graphics.\n\nMetal Gear Solid (1998) brought the series international fame and sold more than 7 million copies. Its sequels became known for long cutscenes, fourth-wall humour and intricate plots, and Kojima built them around themes he named as gene, meme, scene, sense, peace, revenge and race. Metal Gear Solid 2 (7.03 million copies sold) was changed after the September 11 attacks, and Metal Gear Solid 3 was first intended for the PlayStation 3 before the long wait for the console moved it to the PlayStation 2. The Phantom Pain grossed $179 million on release day. Metal Gear Survive, the first game made after he left Konami, sold only a fraction of The Phantom Pain\'s copies. The series had sold 66.4 million units by the end of June 2026.',
    titleKeyword: 'Metal Gear',
    developerKeyword: 'Konami',
