    id: 'tomb-raider',
    name: 'Tomb Raider',
    developer: 'Core Design / Eidos',
    since: 1996,
    description: 'Core Design\'s 1996 game made Lara Croft one of gaming\'s most recognisable characters. Annual sequels strained the studio, Crystal Dynamics took over after 2003, and the series has sold more than 100 million units.',
    longDescription: 'Tomb Raider began in 1994 at the British studio Core Design, where Toby Gard was mostly responsible for creating Lara Croft. Gard first imagined the character as a man; co-founder Jeremy Heath-Smith worried that would look derivative of Indiana Jones, so Gard changed the character\'s gender. Released in 1996, first on the Sega Saturn and then on PlayStation and PC, the game was an unexpected commercial success that reversed the bleak finances of its owner, Eidos. Gard lost creative control over the sequel, and staff said he was saddened by the use of Lara\'s sex appeal in marketing. He left in 1997 with co-creator Paul Douglas to found Confounding Factor.\n\nSuccess brought a punishing schedule. A deal with Sony kept the console versions exclusive to PlayStation until 2000, and Core produced a new game every year for four years. Developer Gavin Rummery opposed the split into multiple teams that made this possible, and left. By Tomb Raider: The Last Revelation (1999) the exhausted staff wanted to move on from Lara, and they left her caught in a collapsing pyramid, and Chronicles (2000) was told in flashbacks at her wake. The Angel of Darkness (2003) struggled through development and was considered a failure, which Rummery later put down to burnout from the annual releases.\n\nEidos handed the series to America\'s Crystal Dynamics, which has been its primary developer ever since. Its Tomb Raider: Legend (2006) was the first reboot, followed by the remake Anniversary (2007) and Underworld (2008). Core, sold to Rebellion in 2006, saw its own anniversary remake cancelled and closed in 2010. A second reboot in 2013 began a trilogy completed by Rise of the Tomb Raider (2015) and Shadow of the Tomb Raider (2018), and the rights passed from Square Enix to Embracer Group in 2022. The series had sold more than 100 million units by 2024, and Lara has earned a place on the Walk of Game and in Guinness World Records.',
    titleKeyword: 'Tomb Raider',
    developerKeyword: 'Core Design',
