    id: 'tetris',
    name: 'Tetris',
    developer: 'Alexey Pajitnov / The Tetris Company',
    since: 1984,
    description: 'Soviet researcher Alexey Pajitnov made Tetris in his spare time and it spread on floppy disks. It became the Game Boy\'s killer app, the prize in a famous rights battle and, by its owner\'s count, the best-selling game ever.',
    longDescription: 'Alexey Pajitnov was a speech recognition and artificial intelligence researcher at the Dorodnitsyn Computing Center of the Soviet Academy of Sciences, and he made games in his spare time. After buying a pentomino puzzle set, he adapted it for the computer, then cut the twelve shapes down to seven tetrominoes because pentominoes made the game needlessly complicated. He wrote the first version in about three weeks on an Elektronika 60, which had no graphics, drawing the pieces with brackets and spaces; it had no score and no levels. The name joined "tetra" with tennis, his favourite sport. With colleague Dmitry Pavlovsky and 16-year-old Vadim Gerasimov he then spent two months moving it to the IBM PC. Soviet law let him earn nothing from the game, and copies passed freely on tapes and floppy disks.\n\nGetting it out of the USSR became one of gaming\'s great legal tangles. Robert Stein of Andromeda Software saw Tetris in Budapest in 1986, read a noncommittal telex from Pajitnov as a licence, and sold the rights to Mirrorsoft and Spectrum HoloByte for £3,000 and royalties before any deal existed. Their releases came out in January 1988, with Spectrum HoloByte playing up the game\'s Soviet origins. The state agency Elorg then signed with Stein, but only for computers. In February 1989 Henk Rogers arrived at Elorg uninvited, and Nintendo left Moscow with the handheld and console rights. Atari Games\' Tengen released an NES version regardless in May 1989, but withdrew it after a US judge rejected its claim to the rights, leaving thousands of cartridges unsold.\n\nThe Game Boy version, a pack-in in the US from 31 July 1989, became the handheld\'s killer app and the best-selling version of the game, with over 35 million sales. Pajitnov emigrated to Seattle in 1991. After Nintendo\'s licence expired in 1995, Rogers and Pajitnov made a formal agreement on 1 January 1996, and Rogers and Elorg\'s Belikov set up The Tetris Company to avoid litigation; it now enforces the game\'s design guidelines and pursues clones. Tetris entered the World Video Game Hall of Fame in its first class in 2015 and reached the cinema as a drama in 2023. The Tetris Company counts 520 million copies sold, most of them paid mobile downloads, though the BBC disputes the title of best-selling game because the figure combines different versions, and argues for Minecraft.',
    titleKeyword: 'Tetris',
    developerKeyword: 'Nintendo',
    sources: [
      { title: 'Tetris', publisher: 'Wikipedia', url: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetris' },
    ],
